    "message": {
     "content": "### 1. The Verdict: Negotiate (Do Not Accept As-Is, Do Not Walk Away)\n\nThis is already a **strong, above-median offer for a Google L6 PM**, but **you should negotiate before making a decision.** \n\nBecause Google reached out to you, you are happy at Meta, and you are leaving behind a stable IC6 trajectory, **you hold maximum leverage**. You can comfortably push for a top-of-band package without risking the offer.\n\n---\n\n### 2. Breakdown: Meta IC6 vs. Google L6\n\n#### Your Current Compensation (Meta IC6)\n* **Base:** $240,000\n* **Target Bonus (15%):** ~$36,000\n* **Equity:** ~$250,000/yr (with $200,000 vesting over the next 12 months)\n* **Total Compensation (TC):** **~$526,000/yr**\n\n---\n\n#### The Google L6 Offer\n* **Base:** $235,000 (posted band: $183,000 \u2013 $271,000)\n* **Target Bonus (20%):** $47,000\n* **RSUs ($1.08M over 4 years):**\n  * *Even split (25%/yr):* $270,000/yr\n  * *Actual Google front-loaded vesting (standard 33% / 33% / 22% / 12% or 38% / 32% / 20% / 10%):*\n    * **Year 1:** ~$356,000 \u2013 $410,000 in equity\n    * **Year 2:** ~$345,000 \u2013 $356,000 in equity\n    * **Year 3:** ~$216,000 \u2013 $238,000 in equity\n    * **Year 4:** ~$108,000 \u2013 $130,000 in equity\n* **Sign-on Bonus:** $0\n\n#### Total Comp Comparison\n\n| Metric | Meta IC6 (Current) | Google L6 (Offer) | Notes |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Year 1 TC** | ~$526,000 | **~$638,000 \u2013 $692,000** | Front-loaded GSUs give you a massive Year 1 boost. |\n| **4-Year Average TC** | ~$526,000 | **~$552,000** | Straight annualized average ($235k + $47k + $270k). |\n| **Base Salary** | **$240,000** | $235,000 | Google offer is slightly below your current base. |\n\n---\n\n### 3. Key Observations & Risks\n\n1. **The \"Year 3/4 Cliff\":** Google's front-loaded vesting means your baseline equity drops significantly in Years 3 and 4. While annual refresh grants do stack, they are tied to performance ratings and are not guaranteed to fully offset the drop.\n2. **Base Salary is Lower:** Leaving a role for a lower base salary ($235k vs. $240k) when the posted band goes up to $271k is unnecessary.\n3. **No Sign-on Bonus:** Google frequently uses sign-on bonuses at L6 ($50k\u2013$100k+) to bridge transition costs, unvested equity, or forfeited bonuses.\n\n---\n\n### 4. What Exactly to Ask For\n\n| Component | Current Offer | Target Counter-Offer | Rationale |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Base Salary** | $235,000 | **$255,000 \u2013 $260,000** | Closes in on top of band ($271k); prevents taking a step down from Meta's $240k. |\n| **Sign-on Bonus** | $0 | **$75,000 \u2013 $100,000** | Compensates for walking away from upcoming Meta vesting and bonus timing. |\n| **Equity (4-Yr)** | $1,080,000 | **$1,200,000** | Pushes annualized equity to $300k/yr ($1.2M total). |\n\nIf Google meets you anywhere near these numbers, your **Year 1 TC will be ~$750,000+** and your 4-year average will sit around **~$600,000/yr**.\n\n---\n\n### 5. Negotiation Script / Strategy\n\nCall or email your recruiter and anchor on enthusiasm for the team, but be direct about the compensation gap.\n\n#### Suggested Talking Points:\n> *\"I really enjoyed meeting the team, and the scope of this L6 role is exciting. However, since I wasn't actively looking and have a very strong setup at Meta, I took a close look at the numbers.*\n> \n> *Right now, my current base is $240k with consistent equity vesting, whereas the offer puts my base at $235k. Additionally, with my upcoming vesting milestones and bonus cycle at Meta, leaving without a sign-on leaves significant value on the table.*\n> \n> *To make this an easy decision for me to sign, here is what would get me to accept immediately:*\n> * *A base salary of **$255,000** (aligned with the upper end of the role\u2019s posted range)*\n> * *A sign-on bonus of **$85,000** to bridge unvested equity and timing differences*\n> * *An equity grant of **$1,200,000** over 4 years*\n> \n> *If we can get to these numbers, I\u2019m ready to sign and wrap up the process.\"*\n\n---\n\n### 6. Summary Recommendation\n* **Do not sign right now.**\n* **Present the counter-proposal above.** Google recruiters expect L6 candidates to negotiate, and their first offer rarely sits at the top of their approval threshold.\n* If they move on the **sign-on bonus** and bump **base/equity**, it becomes an exceptional financial upgrade over Meta. If they refuse to budge entirely, you can comfortably stay at Meta without regret.",
